We have a large snapshot. The task: "Long-context restore/bootstrap analysis with repeated incident logs and repeated runtime files. Identify the highest-risk correctness bug. Explain root cause, smallest safe fix, exact files to change. End with regression checks."

We need to read the snapshot: there are multiple files: GameRuntimeServer.ts, stateStore.ts, replay.ts, types.ts, contentStore.ts, protocol messages, test contract, and many logs (restore-incident.log). The logs show repeated incidents: "restoreSession loaded simulation tick=1245 agents=3 ... phase=running preparedSeed=null connected=false threadIds={} WARN scheduler skipped agent turn because gameplay thread missing WARN client observed session.state phase=running while no thread ids were present". This indicates that after restoreSession, the server is in phase=running but threadIds are empty, causing scheduler to skip agent turns because gameplay thread missing. This is a correctness bug: after restoring a session, the thread IDs are not being set correctly, leading to scheduler skipping turns and possibly deadlock.

We need to identify highest-risk correctness bug: likely in restoreSession method of GameRuntimeServer. The restoreSession loads saved state, sets simulation, seed, threadIds, etc. It seems to be missing the step to restore thread IDs for gameplay agents and builder thread. In restoreSession, after loading saved simulation, they set this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} }; and then they clear threadToAgentId, etc. They also call resetAgentLoopState with agents from saved simulation. However, they do not request threads for existing agents (like they do in startSession). In startSession, after simulation is created, they request threads for each agent and builder. In restoreSession, they only set simulation and agentConfigs but do not start threads for existing agents. This leads to threadIds being empty, causing scheduler to skip turns because nextSchedulableAgentId checks threadIds.gameplayByAgentId[agent.id] existence. Indeed, nextSchedulableAgentId filters agents where this.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId[agent.id] exists. If missing, it will skip.

Thus the bug: restoreSession does not reinitialize thread IDs for existing agents after loading snapshot, causing scheduler to think there are no gameplay threads, leading to warnings and possibly deadlock.

The fix: In restoreSession, after setting simulation and resetting agent loop state, we need to request threads for each agent (and builder) similar to startSession's logic. However we must be careful to not duplicate code. The smallest safe fix: add a method to restore threads for existing agents, maybe call startThreadsForExistingSession (which already exists) after simulation is set. In startSession they call startThreadsForExistingSession after ensuring simulation and thread IDs. In restoreSession they should also call startThreadsForExistingSession after resetting simulation and agent loop state. But startThreadsForExistingSession expects simulation to exist and threadIds to be set? It sets threadIds based on simulation agents. It also sets threadToAgentId. It also sends session state and world snapshot. In restoreSession they already set phase to "running". They could call startThreadsForExistingSession after resetting simulation and before sending session state? Actually startThreadsForExistingSession also sends session state and world snapshot. In restoreSession they later send session state after restoring. Might be okay.

But we need to ensure thread IDs are set before scheduler runs. The scheduler runs via tick and scheduler timer. In restoreSession they set phase to "running" and then later they will have tick timer already running? In start() they call startTimers after restoreSession. In restoreSession they are called from start() after startSession? Actually start() calls restoreSession after content store load and before startTimers? Let's see: start() does: await this.contentStore.load(); await fs.mkdir(...); await this.replay.start(...); await this.logReplay(...); await this.restoreSession(); this.startTimers(); So after restoreSession, timers start. So if restoreSession doesn't set thread IDs, scheduler will skip. So we need to set thread IDs in restoreSession.

Thus the fix: In restoreSession, after resetting simulation and resetting agent loop state, we should call this.startThreadsForExistingSession() (or similar) to request threads for each agent and builder. However startThreadsForExistingSession expects simulation to exist and will request threads for each agent and builder. It also sets threadIds and threadToAgentId. It also logs and sends state. But we already will send session state later? In restoreSession after that they set this.phase = "running"; this.preparedSeed = null; this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} }; this.threadToAgentId.clear(); ... Actually they set threadIds to empty before calling startThreadsForExistingSession? In current code, they set threadIds = { gameplayById: {} } after resetting simulation? Let's examine restoreSession code:

```
private async restoreSession(): Promise<void> {
  const saved = await this.stateStore.load();
  if (!saved) {
    return;
  }

  const content = this.contentStore.getContent();
  if (saved.simulation) {
    this.simulation = Simulation.fromState(saved.simulation, content);
    this.seed = saved.simulation.seed;
    for (const agent of saved.simulation.agents) {
      this.agentConfigs.set(agent.id, { id: agent.id, name: agent.name });
    }
    this.resetAgentLoopState((saved.simulation.agents ?? []).map((agent) => agent.id));
  } else if (typeof saved.seed === "number") {
    this.simulation = new Simulation(saved.seed, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, content, [
      { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" }
    ]);
    this.seed = saved.seed;
    this.agentConfigs.set("agent-1", { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" });
    this.resetAgentLoopState(["agent-1"]);
  } else {
    return;
  }

  this.phase = "running";
  this.preparedSeed = null;
  this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} };
  this.threadToAgentId.clear();
  this.activeTurn = null;
  this.schedulerIndex = 0;
  await this.logReplay("session.restored", {
    seed: this.seed,
    tick: this.simulation.getState().tick
  });
}
```

We see they set threadIds to empty before logging. They do not request threads. So we need to request threads for each agent and builder after resetting simulation and before setting thread

